{"name":"Dark Topaz","hex":"#817c87","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an absolute or (more often) relative lack of light.","Extinguished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A complete or (more often) partial absence of light.","Ignorance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dark"},{"term":"topaz","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A silicate mineral of aluminium and fluorine, usually tinted by impurities.","An often clear, yellowish-brown gemstone cut from this."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellowish-brown color, like that of the gemstone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/topaz"}],"entries":[{"title":"Topaz","blocks":[{"text":"Topaz is a silicate mineral made of aluminum and fluorine with the chemical formula Al2SiO4(F, OH)2. It is used as a gemstone in jewelry and other adornments. Common topaz in its natural state is colorless, though trace element impurities can make it pale blue or golden-brown to yellow-orange. Topaz is often treated with heat or radiation to make it a deep blue, reddish-orange, pale green, pink, or purple."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg/330px-Topaze%2C_quartz_fum%C3%A9_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Topaz"},{"title":"Darkness","blocks":[{"text":"Darkness is the condition resulting from a lack of illumination, or an absence of visible light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Creation_of_Light.png/330px-Creation_of_Light.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darkness"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Topaz.GIF/500px-Topaz.GIF?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","sr